  "assessment": "This change introduces a new `Counter` struct wrapping a `Mutex<u64>`, but the `increment` method explicitly releases its lock between reading the current value and writing back the incremented value. The added `std::thread::yield_now()` call forces context switches that allow other threads to read stale state before the write occurs. Consequently, this change fails to fix the concurrency bug and actively guarantees lost updates during concurrent access.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "actual": "The final count is significantly lower than the total number of calls made, with values frequently diverging by hundreds or thousands depending on thread scheduling.",
      "at": "src/lib.rs:19-23",
      "call": "Spawn 8 threads, each calling `counter.increment()` 10,000 times concurrently. After all threads join, check if `counter.get() == 80_000`.

```rust
// Example trigger command/test setup
let counter = Arc::new(Counter::new());
let mut handles = vec![];
for _ in 0..8 {
    let c = Arc::clone(&counter);
    handles.push(std::thread::spawn(move || {
        for _ in 0..10_000 { c.increment(); }
    }));
}
for h in handles { h.join().unwrap(); }
assert_eq!(counter.get(), 80_000);
```",
      "defect": "Lost Updates in `increment` due to Unprotected Read/Write Gap",
      "expected": "The final count matches exactly the sum of all increments performed across threads."
    }
  ]
